Through our analysis, approximately around 13% of store-weeks had sales at a rate at least 10% above their store's average. In terms of the probability for a strong week, we found that Thanksgiving had the highest probability, with around 82%. On the other hand, November or December had a strong association with higher sales. Additionally, the holiday-only staffing rule had around a 27% precision, missing around 85% of the strong weeks in the database. This made it less effective than staffing throughout November or December. For our Naive Bayes model accuracy, it achieved around 87% accuracy but failing to identify any strong weeks. This means that despite the high accuracy, it was not able to outperform the holiday rule for identifying busy weeks. Additionally, the data for 2012 test data did not have Thanksgiving and Christmas, which could affect some accuracy in holiday-related sales patterns.
